The ceremony ended and the room went back to being a bar.\
It did not thin out. Nothing in the Clambake ever thinned out; it only redistributed, and it redistributed now into a slow churn between the spa bath and the bar, with a hard eddy where the two currents met and nobody could get through holding a full glass. The staircase was occupied to the turn. The mezzanine was occupied. There were Elvi up there who had not been able to see any of it and had cheered every word.\
It was a wake and it was a coronation and nobody had told the room which one to be, so it was being both, a few metres apart. Two Blue Hawaiis were laughing so hard that one had to take the other\'92s sleeve to stay upright. Close enough to touch them, a Tulsa wept into a beer with somebody\'92s hand flat on the back of his neck, and neither party found the other odd.\
Sarah stayed where she was, because standing up was a project with stages and an audience, and because there was no need to. The room came past her. They came in ones and twos with their hands already half out, and every second one wanted to touch the belly, and about a third asked first.\
Kathy went into it.\
She had not come with a plan. She had come to a wake with a bag she had not opened, and there was a thing at the back of her head that had been sitting there since Bent Street and had never once arranged itself into a question. It was less a suspicion than a set of pieces in a drawer: what she had found in the blood of the dead, and what she had never found in the blood of anybody who was well, on account of nobody who is well ever giving you any.\
The room was full of people who were well.\
So she circulated, in the way a woman circulates at a party, and what she was actually doing was getting sleeves pushed up.\
A Clambake with grey coming through his sideburns got himself off the chesterfield in three separate movements, with a hand on the arm of it and a sound. Two paces further on, an Elvine came up off a bar stool and stood a moment with her palm pressed flat into the small of her back, waiting for something to finish. Nobody looked at either of them. In that room it was weather.\
Then Rusty Martin came off a low stool in one go, still talking, and went to the bar.\
Kathy did not stop walking. Her head turned, though, and stayed turned a beat past where it had any business.\
Maile Duval had her sleeve pushed up past the elbow and her arm out and a small audience for it, and she was delighted.\
\'93[ ] years,\'94 she was saying. \'93[ ] years I\'92ve had that, and I went to a man in Bent Street about getting it taken off and he told me it\'92d cost more than the wedding did.\'94\
\'93Let me,\'94 said Kathy.\
Maile put the arm into her hands with the satisfaction of a woman who has finally found somebody qualified to agree with her. There had been a name on the inside of the forearm once, done in the ornate lettering that takes a good hand and a long sitting. It was a grey suggestion now. The heavy work at the edges had held on better than the rest, so what was left was an outline with the middle gone out of it, like a word rubbed off a blackboard by somebody in a hurry.\
\'93When did it start going?\'94\
\'93That\'92s what I can\'92t tell you. I looked at his name every morning of my life and then one morning I\'92m at the sink and I think, hang on.\'94 She turned the wrist over, pleased with herself. \'93Best thing that\'92s happened to me all year, and I\'92m counting getting out of Bent Street.\'94\
Kathy turned the arm slightly, into the light off the bar, and did not let go of it.\
\'93Any scars?\'94\
\'93What?\'94\
\'93Anything you\'92ve had a long time. Old ones.\'94\
Maile thought about it, and then pushed the sleeve the rest of the way up, to a place above the elbow where there was nothing whatever to see, and looked at the nothing for a while with a different face on.\
\'93Huh,\'94 she said.\
Kathy gave the arm back. She did it gently, and she was already looking past her.\
It was not the same on all of them. The woman beside Maile Duval had a forearm you could still read. Dianne Carter, two along, had one you would have to be told was there. Some of them were further into it than others, and there was nothing in any of their faces to say which.\
Kathy came back to the dais with nothing in her hands and sat down next to Hayley, who had not moved and had not written anything down.\
\'93Say something to me,\'94 said Kathy.\
\'93About what?\'94\
\'93About that lot. I want to hear it out loud and I don\'92t want to be the one who says it first.\'94\
\'93They were at Bent Street,\'94 said Hayley.\
\'93So were a lot of people who aren\'92t here.\'94\
\'93They were at Bent Street, and they\'92re the only ones in this room you\'92d look at twice.\'94\
\'93It isn\'92t pathogenic,\'94 Kathy said. \'93That\'92s what I keep coming back to. Whatever\'92s in them isn\'92t making them ill. It\'92s the other thing. It\'92s salutogenic.\'94\
Hayley looked at her.\
\'93It\'92s a word,\'94 said Kathy.\
\'93I\'92m sure it is.\'94\
\'93It means it goes through you and tidies up.\'94 She put both hands flat on her knees. \'93Something\'92s in them, it\'92s been in some of them longer than others, and it did not get there out of the air.\'94\
\'93Who\'92s got what?\'94 said Chanta, from the edge of the dais, with an Elvisette working on his shoulders and a beer he had not asked for.\
Nobody answered him.\
Hayley turned round slowly and looked at what was behind him. Betty Lee\'92s hair had gone entirely back to blonde at the roots. Marguerita, who had complained about that knee for [ ], had done the splits at a party and had not yet stopped telling people about it.\
\'93How long have they been with you?\'94 said Hayley.\
\'93Depends which.\'94 Chanta brightened, pleased to be asked something he could answer. \'93Betty\'92s ages. Marguerita, ooh. Bit less.\'94\
\'93And the ones by the curtains. Dianne and that lot.\'94\
\'93Yeah, they\'92re lovely.\'94\
\'93How lovely?\'94\
\'93Very lovely.\'94\
\'93Chanta.\'94\
\'93They\'92re very lovely,\'94 said Chanta, with the emphasis of a man being asked to repeat himself for no reason he could see, and then, because he was constitutionally unable to leave a silence alone, and because a corner of him had spent his whole life waiting for somebody in authority to ask him a question he could answer, he filled it. \'93All of them, if you want the truth. Every one of them. I\'92m not proud of it. Well. I\'92m a bit proud of it.\'94\
Nobody said anything.\
It took about two seconds, which in a room that size is a long time. Hayley got there first, because it was her job. Kathy got there next and put both hands over her mouth. Sarah, from the chair, turned her head very slowly towards the curtains.\
Walter got there before Chanta did, and Walter was already grinning like a split melon.\
Chanta looked round at the faces.\
\'93What,\'94 he said.\
Then, a moment later: \'93What?\'94\
From the chair, without turning round, Sarah said: \'93Then it can be moved.\'94\
She said it the way a person says a thing they have just this second thought of. She put no particular weight on it at all. She was looking at the curtains and not at anybody.\
Chanta looked at Kathy. Kathy did not look back at him, which was as close to mercy as she got.\
He was not quick, and everybody present had arranged their expectations accordingly, and so nobody was watching him closely enough to see him do it.\
\'93Oh,\'94 said Chanta.\
\'93Chanta \'97\'94\
\'93No, hang on.\'94 He put the beer down on the dais without looking where. \'93Hang on.\'94\
\'93It is not that simple.\'94\
\'93It\'92s exactly that simple,\'94 said Chanta, with the terrible confidence of a man who has arrived at the correct answer by a route nobody else can follow. \'93You\'92ve just been saying it wrong.\'94\
He stood up, and went up the two wooden steps onto the spa bath in front of the mermaids, in the cape and the sash and the crown, and the room turned over to look at him, because the room always did.\
He did not do the ceremonial voice.\
\'93Brothers and sisters,\'94 said Chanta. \'93I\'92ve got good news.\'94\
They waited. They had waited like this once before, in this room, for a man standing on this same bath, and that man had asked them to sweep the streets.\
\'93The Holy Aunts have worked out what\'92s been killing us,\'94 Chanta said. \'93That\'92s not the good news. The good news is they\'92ve worked out what stops it, and it\'92s already here, and some of you have got it already and don\'92t know you have.\'94\
Nobody moved.\
\'93It\'92s rooting,\'94 said Chanta.\
The Clambake did not make a sound.\
\'93Sex,\'94 he said, in case the theological ones needed it spelled out. \'93It\'92s sex. That\'92s the whole of it. Whatever it is, it\'92s going round the way that goes round, it\'92s been going round a while, and everyone it gets to is one it doesn\'92t get.\'94 He opened his arms, and the cape opened with him, and the light came off the rhinestones and went round the room like something being handed out. \'93So there\'92s my first decree, and it\'92s the only one I\'92ve got. Go forth and hump.\'94
